@*
    REQ-UI-009 — the dark-first theme toggle.

    TrBlazeUI has no theme-toggle component, so this is the documented workaround: a `Switch` between a
    sun and a moon `LucideIcon` that flips `<html class="dark">` and persists the choice in the
    `tflens:theme` cookie, which App.razor reads before the first byte of the next request.
*@
@implements IDisposable
@inject ShellPreferences objPreferences

@*
    REQ-UI-009, fixed 2026-08-29 — moon + switch, which is what every mockup draws (the left panel of
    `docs/mockups/login.html`, the sidebar footer of `coverage.html`). The build had sun + switch + moon.
    The leading sun was not only extra: at 1280 the header needs 1090px of a 1024px bar on the six report
    routes, and those 22px were part of why the breadcrumb had no room and the row wrapped (REQ-UI-010).
    The switch itself still states both states to a screen reader through its label.

    Fix cycle 1, 2026-09-11 — drawn to the mockups' `<button class="switch">` control for control:
    * the glyph follows the theme — a sun on the light palette, a moon on the dark one. The mockups carry
      both and hide one (`html.dark .switch .sun{display:none}`); so does this, from app.css, so the right
      glyph is on the first byte whatever the circuit has read yet. Only one is ever visible, so the
      width the REQ-UI-010 budget above counted is unchanged.
    * the track is the mockup's 36×20 (`.switch .trk`), which is TrBlazeUI's `SwitchSize.Small`.
    * the test id sits on the whole control — glyph and track — which is where every mockup hangs
      `theme-toggle`. It used to sit on the track alone, leaving the glyph outside the anchored node (the
      anchoring waiver in tests/verify/_mockup-parity.ts names exactly this move as the way to lift it).

    2026-09-12, cluster C's `theme-toggle > span[0]` positional-icon finding — the sr-only `<Label>` is
    gone. The mockups' control has exactly three children (`svg.sun`, `svg.moon`, `span.trk`) and this
    had four, with a 1×1 `<label>` at index 0 (measured: `label` 1×1, `svg` 0×0, `svg` 16×16, `button`
    36×20). Every glyph was therefore one position out against the mockup, which is what the gate saw.

    Nothing accessible is lost, and nothing was ever gained: `Switch` carries `AriaLabel="Dark mode"`,
    and `aria-label` WINS over an associated `<label for>` in the accessible-name computation, so the
    element only ever added a node to the DOM. The control still announces itself as "Dark mode", and
    `role="switch"` still states on/off. `Id` stays on the Switch — the tests address it.
*@
<div class="tflens-theme-toggle flex items-center gap-2" data-testid="@TestId">
    <LucideIcon Name="sun" Size="16" Class="tflens-theme-sun text-muted-foreground" />
    <LucideIcon Name="moon" Size="16" Class="tflens-theme-moon text-muted-foreground" />
    <Switch Id="@Id"
            Size="SwitchSize.Small"
            Checked="@objPreferences.IsDark"
            CheckedChanged="OnCheckedChangedAsync"
            AriaLabel="Dark mode" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The <c>data-testid</c> the toggle carries; the header's is <c>theme-toggle</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string TestId { get; set; } = "theme-toggle";

    /// <summary>The switch's element id, so its screen-reader label can point at it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Id { get; set; } = "theme-toggle-switch";

    /// <summary>
    /// Subscribes to the shared preference so the header and sidebar toggles never disagree.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        objPreferences.Changed += OnPreferencesChanged;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Applies the new choice to the document and the cookie.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="aIsDark"><c>true</c> when the user switched to the dark palette.</param>
    /// <returns>A task that completes once the browser has been told.</returns>
    private async Task OnCheckedChangedAsync(bool aIsDark)
    {
        await objPreferences.SetThemeAsync(aIsDark);
    }

    /// <summary>Re-renders when the other toggle changed the preference.</summary>
    private void OnPreferencesChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    /// <summary>Unsubscribes so the circuit does not leak a handler per navigation.</summary>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        objPreferences.Changed -= OnPreferencesChanged;
    }
}
